+# Distributed budgets: what a cluster costs, as counts
+
+"Does it scale?" is not a question with a yes. It is a question with a **budget**: how many messages a
+write costs, how many acknowledgements decide it, how much of that work each node does, and how many
+round trips a caller waits for. Every one of those is a count, and a count is the same number on a
+busy machine as on an idle one — which is why this project budgets in counts and not in milliseconds.
+
+## The three numbers
+
+| number | a cluster of `n` nodes |
+| --- | --- |
+| quorum | `⌊n/2⌋ + 1` — what decides anything, in every step of this track |
+| fan-out | `n − 1` — how many messages the leader sends per write |
+| acknowledgements | `quorum − 1` — how many the leader waits for (it counts itself) |
+
+The fan-out and the acknowledgements are different numbers, and both matter: the first is what the
+network carries, the second is what the write waits for. A budget that used one for the other would
+either overstate the network or overstate the latency.
+
+## The shape
+
+For one thousand writes:
+
+| cluster | messages | acks | leader messages | one follower | per-node average |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| 3 nodes | 2 000 | 1 000 | 2 000 | 1 000 | 667 |
+| 5 nodes | 4 000 | 2 000 | 4 000 | 1 000 | 800 |
+| 7 nodes | 6 000 | 3 000 | 6 000 | 1 000 | 857 |
+
+The **leader's** work per write is `n − 1` messages — one per peer; a **follower's** is one, whatever
+`n` is. So as the cluster grows the total grows, and the average per node grows with it — because the
+leader's share grows with the cluster and a follower's does not. That is the shape every replicated system has, and it is
+why "add a node" is not free: it buys redundancy and costs the leader a message per write.
+
+## Round trips
+
+A write is decided in one round trip (send, wait for the quorum), whatever `n` is; a linearizable read
+(`5.6`) is one round too. Those are the numbers a latency budget is made of, and they are counts of
+hops rather than milliseconds, so the budget does not move with the machine.
+
+## What the gate reads
+
+`bench-budget` reads the counts `tests/budget_distributed.rs` prints and compares them with
+`budget/5.17-distributed-budgets.toml`. The baseline is the model's own arithmetic at `n = 5`, one
+thousand writes — so a change that adds a message per write (a new hop, an extra message kind) shows
+up as a number that moved, and a change that only renames things does not.
